#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9598539e-b54f-5c35-85a9-860683f593e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9598539e-b54f-5c35-85a9-860683f593e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286195} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c5e5c8a-f0ab-52e4-a5aa-56854d500dd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b038fbb-5dcb-597f-a35d-5322f573b512" :question/math-academy-id "q-319254" :question/problem "Let $p=3i+2j-k$ and $q=-2i+xj.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -6+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
